<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mare Liberum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1609</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mare Liberum ('The Free Sea') is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> treatise by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-grotius">Hugo Grotius</a>, published in 1609, arguing that the sea is international territory open to the ships of all nations. Written in defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>'s right to trade in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> against the Portuguese claim to a monopoly, it became a founding text of the law of the sea. It provoked replies such as John Selden's Mare Clausum (1635).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mare-liberum.jpg" alt="Mare Liberum" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hugo Grotius. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HugoGrotius-MareLiberum-1609.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
